---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const toneCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'a*sin(2*pi*f*x)', label: 'your wave', color: '#d946ef', width: 2.2 },
    { expr: '0.5*sin(2*pi*2*f*x)', label: 'one octave up (2f)', color: 'rgba(23,36,32,0.6)' },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'a', label: 'amplitude (loudness)', min: 0.1, max: 1, step: 0.05, value: 0.6 },
    { name: 'f', label: 'frequency f (Hz)', min: 110, max: 880, step: 5, value: 220, unit: 'Hz' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 0.02, label: 'time (s)' },
  y: { min: -1, max: 1 },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/music/foundational/sound-and-music">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Music is physics you can dance to. Every sound is air pressure oscillating; every note is an
    oscillation at a steady <strong>frequency</strong>, counted in hertz (Hz = waves per second).
    The international tuning note, A above middle C, is <strong>440 Hz</strong>. Pitch is
    frequency as your ear hears it; loudness is amplitude; <strong>timbre</strong> — what makes a
    violin and a flute playing the same note sound different — is the shape of the wave on top
    of the pure tone.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="a live tone — raise f for pitch, a for volume; the grey wave is one octave above yours" widget="plotter" config={toneCfg} />

  <p>
    Slide the frequency and count the cycles squeezed into those 20 milliseconds. Then notice the
    grey curve completes exactly two cycles for your one — that relationship, a clean 2:1, is the
    <strong>octave</strong>, the only interval every musical culture on Earth treats as "the same
    note, higher".
  </p>

  <h2>The arithmetic of harmony</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pythagoras, ~550 BCE:</strong> strings at 2:1 sound sweetly related (octave), 3:2 even sweeter (the perfect fifth). Simple ratios = consonance; ugly ratios = tension. Western music is built on that discovery.</li>
    <li><strong>Equal temperament:</strong> the octave split into 12 equal steps, each a factor of 2^(1/12) ≈ 1.0595 — so every key sounds equally in tune. Zhu Zaiyu computed it first (1584); Europe followed; Bach's <em>Well-Tempered Clavier</em> (1722) toured the result.</li>
    <li><strong>Loudness is logarithmic:</strong> each +10 dB is roughly "twice as loud" and ten times the energy — whisper 30 dB, conversation 60, hearing-damage territory from ~85 dB of sustained exposure.</li>
    <li><strong>Rhythm is arithmetic too</strong> — time signatures are fractions (4/4 = four quarter-note beats), tempo is beats per minute, and polyrhythms are two fractions agreeing to disagree until they meet again.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The deep takeaway: consonance and rhythm are <em>predictions</em> your brain enjoys — music
    is applied pattern-recognition, which is why the physics of sound and the psychology of
    listening keep meeting in the middle.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ISO 16 (standard pitch); Benson, Music: A Mathematical Offering; Zhu Zaiyu's equal-temperament calculation (1584).</p>
</TopicPage>
